# 6. Lesson 1 --- Start With Structure

Before styling anything, build the HTML skeleton.

Think:

``` text
body
└── wrapper
    └── 600px email container
        ├── header
        ├── welcome section
        ├── offer section
        ├── services section
        ├── repair CTA
        └── footer
```

### Why this matters

Email development becomes much easier when you can look at the HTML and
immediately understand what each section does.

Do not build everything as one giant block.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

# 7. Lesson 2 --- 600px Email Container

Continue using the controlled desktop email width learned in Project 1.

Target:

``` html
width="600"
```

and/or:

``` css
max-width:600px;
```

The important lesson is not simply memorising `600px`.

A controlled container makes the layout more predictable across email
clients and gives Outlook a reliable table-based structure.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

# 8. Lesson 3 --- Outlook / MSO Structure

We are continuing the ghost-table lesson from Project 1.

Example:

``` html
<!--[if mso]>
<table role="presentation" width="600" border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0">
<tr>
<td>
<![endif]-->

<!-- Modern email content -->

<!--[if mso]>
</td>
</tr>
</table>
<![endif]-->
```

### Mental model

Modern email clients see the modern HTML.

Outlook receives the MSO fallback structure.

We are building **one design with two compatible ways of describing its
layout**.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

# 9. Lesson 4 --- Keep Modern and Outlook Layouts Aligned

One of the most important lessons from Project 1:

If the modern version says:

``` text
3 columns
```

the Outlook version should also describe:

``` text
3 columns
```

If the modern version says:

``` text
50% + 50%
```

the Outlook version should not randomly say:

``` text
33% + 33%
```

### Rule

> **Count the columns. Make the modern and MSO structures describe the
> same layout.**

